# C# Unit Test Conventions (LAP)

Apply these rules for unit test files.

**Namespaces:** Prefer **file-scoped** `namespace ...;` and a blank line before the first type in the file (same as the **File-scoped namespaces** section in `csharp-code-style.mdc`).

## 1) AAA structure is mandatory

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Test_Example()
{
    // Arrange

    // Act

    // Assert
}
```

- Keep all three comments in exact order.
- Even if a section is short, keep the section comment.

## 2) Dependency doubles: NSubstitute

- Use `Substitute.For<T>()` for interface dependencies.
- SUT is usually the only real implementation.
- Avoid handwritten fakes unless explicitly needed.

```csharp
var userRepository = Substitute.For<IUserRepository>();
var sut = new UserService(userRepository);
```

## 3) HttpClient mocking

- If code uses HttpClient, return a real client with `BaseAddress`.

```csharp
var httpService = Substitute.For<IHttpService>();
var httpClient = new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri("https://test.com") };
httpService.GetClient().Returns(httpClient);
```

## 4) Verify behavior with `Received` / `DidNotReceive`

```csharp
userRepository.Received(1).Save(Arg.Any<User>());
userRepository.Received(1).Delete(userId);
userRepository.DidNotReceive().Update(Arg.Any<User>());
```

- Use `Arg.Is<T>(...)` when you need value/content matching.
- If production creates a new list (`ToList()`), verify with `Arg.Is` + `SequenceEqual`.

## 5) Test naming and order

- Naming pattern: `MethodName_ShouldExpectedBehavior_WhenCondition` — every test name should include a `_When…` clause (even if long) so the scenario is obvious at a glance.

```csharp
public void GetUser_ShouldReturnNull_WhenUserNotFound()
public void CreateOrder_ShouldThrowException_WhenProductIsOutOfStock()
```

- Keep class test order aligned with the **production type** (constructor first, then public methods in source order — e.g. `CanExecute` before `Execute`):
  1. **Constructor** — all `ArgumentNullException` / guard tests first; no method tests before these finish.
  2. **Each public method (in source order)** — for that method only:
     - **first:** null-check and other guard/throw tests (`ThrowIfNull`, invalid input, etc.)
     - **then:** happy-path / behavior tests (`ShouldReturn…`, `ShouldCall…`, `Received`, etc.)
  3. Repeat step 2 for the next method (`CanExecute` block fully done — guards then behavior — before any `Execute` test).

```text
Constructor_ShouldThrow…_When…           ← all constructor guards
CanExecute_ShouldThrow…_When…            ← CanExecute guards first
CanExecute_ShouldReturn…_When…           ← then CanExecute behavior
Execute_ShouldThrow…_When…               ← Execute guards first
Execute_ShouldCall…_When…                ← then Execute behavior
```

- If production does **not** null-check a method (thin delegate with no guards), skip that method’s guard block and write only its behavior tests — still after prior methods’ full blocks.

## 6) FluentAssertions over classic asserts

```csharp
actual.Should().NotBeNull();
actual.Should().Be(expectedValue);
act.Should().Throw<ArgumentNullException>().WithParameterName("tabService");
act.Should().Throw<InvalidOperationException>();
```

Avoid equivalent `Assert.*` when FluentAssertions covers the same intent.

## 6b) Exception assertions: type only, not message text

- Assert that the **expected exception type** is thrown; do **not** assert exact `Exception.Message` with `WithMessage(...)`.
- Fixed production exception literals are an implementation detail; tests should stay stable when wording changes.
- For `ArgumentNullException` / `ArgumentException`, `WithParameterName(...)` is still allowed to verify **which parameter** failed.
- For domain/business failures (`InvalidOperationException`, `NotSupportedException`, etc.), stop at the type:

```csharp
// Correct
act.Should().Throw<InvalidOperationException>();

// Wrong — couples test to message literal
act.Should().Throw<InvalidOperationException>()
    .WithMessage("Visible element attributes do not contain the instance element.");
```

## 7) Variable naming in tests

- Act result variable: `actual`
- Throwing delegate: `act`
- Expected variables: `expected...`
- No `mock` prefix for substitutes

```csharp
// Act
var actual = sut.GetUserById(1);

// Act for exception
var act = () => sut.DeleteUser(invalidId);
```

## 8) Test data readability

- Use explicit **Guid string literals** parsed with `Guid.Parse("...")` (or `private static readonly Guid` fields initialized from `Guid.Parse`) so tests stay deterministic and readable. Do **not** use `Guid.NewGuid()` in Arrange unless the test explicitly verifies uniqueness behavior.
- Use clear English sample values (`"first-group"`, `"second-group"`) instead of ambiguous placeholders.
- Build needed data inline in each test; avoid extracting private helper methods when it hurts local readability.

## 8b) Assert the full method result

- For behavior tests that return a DTO or collection, build the complete **`expected`** return value in **Arrange**.
- In **Assert**, compare the whole result: **`actual.Should().BeEquivalentTo(expected)`**.
- Do not assert only fragments (a single property, one item, or a side-effect) when the method returns a structured result — unless the test is an exception/guard path with no return value.

## 9) Fixture setup pattern

- Create shared substitutes and `_sut` in test class constructor.
- In each test, configure only scenario-specific returns.

```csharp
public sealed class MyServiceTests
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _userRepository;
    private readonly MyService _sut;

    public MyServiceTests()
    {
        _userRepository = Substitute.For<IUserRepository>();
        _sut = new MyService(_userRepository);
    }
}
```

## 10) `IOptions<T>` behavior-aware testing

- Constructor snapshot style: create a new SUT per options set.
- Re-read style: update `.Value.Returns(...)` in Arrange for that scenario.

```csharp
var optionsDisabled = Options.Create(new FeatureConfig { Enabled = false });
var sutDisabled = new MyFeatureService(optionsDisabled, _dep);

var optionsEnabled = Options.Create(new FeatureConfig { Enabled = true });
var sutEnabled = new MyFeatureService(optionsEnabled, _dep);
```

## 11) Collections: assert by value, not reference

- Compare collections with `BeEquivalentTo(...)`.
- Do not use `BeSameAs`, `NotBeSameAs`, or `ReferenceEquals` for collection assertions.

```csharp
var actual = sut.GetOrderedIds();
var expected = new[] { id1, id2, id3 };

actual.Should().BeEquivalentTo(expected);
```

## 12) Visualizer-specific guidance

- `GraphStruct.Nodes`/`Edges` may return `ToList()`: use `Arg.Is` + `SequenceEqual`.
- `LoadProfilePhotosExecutor`: align verification with list-content match, not reference.
- Use `Guid.Parse("...")` literals for deterministic Visualizer/Explore test data (see section 8).
- Add at least one test for `ApplyProfilePhotosExecutor.ApplyWithResult`, not only `Apply`.
- Remove or implement `TODO` comments in tests.

## 13) CancellationToken and `ITraceInstrumentation` in tests

- When the SUT’s public API takes **`CancellationToken`**, pass **`CancellationToken.None`** (or a **`CancellationTokenSource`** when testing cancellation) into **Act** and include the token in **`Received(...)`** / **`Arg.Any<CancellationToken>()`** assertions when substitutes expose that parameter.
- When the SUT requires **`ITraceInstrumentation`**, construct with **`new TraceInstrumentation()`** from **`LAP.MicroService.IAPService.Commons.Observability`** (real no-op-friendly instrumentation for spans), or **`Substitute.For<ITraceInstrumentation>()`** if the test never asserts on tracing.

## 14) End-to-end example

```csharp
public sealed class UserServiceTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void GetUserById_ShouldReturnUser_WhenUserExists()
    {
        // Arrange
        var userRepository = Substitute.For<IUserRepository>();
        var expectedUser = new User { Id = 1, Name = "Ali" };
        userRepository.GetById(1).Returns(expectedUser);
        var sut = new UserService(userRepository);

        // Act
        var actual = sut.GetUserById(1);

        // Assert
        actual.Should().NotBeNull();
        actual.Should().Be(expectedUser);
        userRepository.Received(1).GetById(1);
    }
}
```

## 14) FluentValidation tests

- Place validator tests under **`LAP.Tests/.../Validators/`**, mirroring production **`Validators/`** layout.
- SUT: **`IValidator<T>`** — usually `new MyValidator()` in the test constructor (same as [`SearchRequestValidatorTest`](MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/LAP.Tests/Facade/Validators/Search/SearchRequestValidatorTest.cs)).
- Act: **`var actual = _sut.Validate(request);`**
- Assert: **`actual.IsValid`** and **`actual.Errors`** should contain expected **`.ErrorCode`** (compare to `public const` on the validator class), not ad-hoc message strings.
- When the consumer soft-fails on invalid input, keep at least one **consumer test** (return null / skip) plus focused **validator tests** for each rule.

## 15) Quick checklist

- AAA comments exist and ordered
- Substitute with `Substitute.For<T>()`
- Verify behavior via `Received` / `DidNotReceive`
- Naming pattern: `Method_Should_When`
- FluentAssertions preferred
- Exception asserts: expected type only; no `WithMessage(...)` (section 6b); `WithParameterName` OK for `ArgumentNullException`
- `actual` / `act` / `expected...` naming
- Guid test data: `Guid.Parse("...")` literals — not `Guid.NewGuid()` (section 8)
- Behavior tests: full `expected` return value + `BeEquivalentTo(expected)` (section 8b)
- Prefer inline data over private test helper methods
- Constructor fixture builds `_sut`
- Collections compared with `BeEquivalentTo`
- `IOptions<T>` tests align with runtime behavior
- No dangling TODO comments
- SUTs with `CancellationToken` / `ITraceInstrumentation`: section 13
- FluentValidation: validator tests assert `ErrorCode` constants (section 14)
